{% sec 1, "Trading the Whole World", "What does a macro fund actually trade?", "Trading the whole world" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Think of a weather forecaster. She doesn't study one tree; she studies pressure systems, ocean temperatures and wind across a whole continent, then says where it'll rain next week. A {% gl "global macro" %}global macro{% endgl %} manager does the same with economies. She reads growth, inflation, jobs, trade, central bank plans, elections and wars, then asks one question: <strong>what does the market have wrong?</strong></p>
    <p>She isn't tied to any one market. If the view is about interest rates she trades bonds; if it's about a country's money she trades its currency; if it's about oil she trades oil. The table shows the five main toolkits.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Market</th><th>What moves it</th><th>How macro funds trade it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Currencies (FX)</td><td>Interest rate gaps between countries, trade balances, money flowing in and out, politics</td><td>Spot and forwards, options. Often the biggest part of the book</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Interest rates</td><td>What central banks will do, inflation, growth</td><td>Government bonds, rate futures, {% gl "interest rate swap" %}swaps{% endgl %}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stock indices</td><td>The whole economy's outlook, not single companies</td><td>Index futures and ETFs (S&amp;P 500, Nikkei, FTSE)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Commodities</td><td>Supply and demand, wars, stockpiles, weather</td><td>Futures and options on oil, gas, gold, copper, crops</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Credit</td><td>Default risk of countries and companies</td><td>{% gl "credit default swap" %}CDS{% endgl %}, bonds, credit indices</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>All five are deep, liquid markets, so a macro fund can put on or take off billions of dollars of risk in a day. That's why macro funds can run very large amounts of money, and why macro is the strategy closest to a bank's FX and rates trading desks: many macro portfolio managers started there.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "Discretionary or Systematic?", "Who makes the decisions: a person or a program?", "Discretionary or systematic" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Macro splits into two very different ways of working.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>{% gl "discretionary" %}Discretionary{% endgl %} macro</th><th>{% gl "systematic" %}Systematic{% endgl %} macro and CTAs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Who decides</td><td>A portfolio manager, using research and judgement</td><td>Computer models, mostly following trends</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Positions</td><td>A few big, high-conviction bets</td><td>Many small positions across dozens of markets</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Holding through pain</td><td>Will sit through big swings if the story still holds</td><td>Rules decide: if the trend turns, the model exits</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Does best when</td><td>A big turning point the market hasn't priced in</td><td>Long, steady trends, including in crises</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Struggles when</td><td>The manager's story is wrong, or right too early</td><td>Choppy markets where trends keep reversing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Famous names</td><td>Soros (Quantum), Druckenmiller (Duquesne), Tudor Jones</td><td>Man AHL, Winton, AQR; Bridgewater runs systematic macro</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Trend-following CTAs get their own note later in this chapter. The rest of this note is mainly about the discretionary kind: one person, a view of the world, and the nerve to bet on it.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "The Macro Framework", "How does a macro manager go from a view to a trade?", "The macro framework" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Deltashark breaks the process into three questions, asked in order. A view is worth nothing until all three have an answer.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="gm-flow" aria-label="The three steps of the macro framework">
    <div><h4>1. Macro analysis</h4><p>Growth, inflation, jobs, trade, central bank policy.<span class="q">What will happen to the economy?</span></p></div>
    <div><h4>2. Market analysis</h4><p>{% gl "positioning" %}Positioning{% endgl %}, money flows, charts, sentiment.<span class="q">Is it already in the price?</span></p></div>
    <div><h4>3. Risk management</h4><p>Position size, correlations, what-if scenarios.<span class="q">How much can I lose if I'm wrong?</span></p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="gm-arrow" aria-hidden="true">▼</p>
  <div class="gm-out" aria-label="Kinds of trade that come out"><span>FX trades</span><span>Rates trades</span><span>Equity index</span><span>Commodities</span><span>Credit</span></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Step two is the one beginners skip. Being right about the economy only pays if most of the market is wrong. If every fund already expects a rate cut and has bet on it, the cut can arrive and prices barely move, or even move the other way as everyone takes profits. Macro traders call this "buy the rumour, sell the fact".</p>
    <p>Step three is about the whole book, not one trade. A macro manager usually decides how much of the fund she's willing to lose on an idea before choosing the instruments. Deltashark's example below risks \(2\%\) of the fund.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 4, "Anatomy of a Trade: Japan, 2022 to 2024", "What does a real macro trade look like, and how did it play out?", "Anatomy of a trade" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>For years the Bank of Japan held its 10-year bond yield near \(0\%\) using {% gl "yield curve control" %}yield curve control{% endgl %} (QE and QT explains how). By late 2022, every other big central bank was raising rates fast, and the BoJ was under strain. Many macro managers concluded the policy couldn't last. Deltashark's version of the trade has three legs:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Leg</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Short</strong> Japanese government bonds (JGBs)</td><td>If the cap goes, yields rise and bond prices fall</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Long</strong> the yen</td><td>Higher Japanese rates should pull money into the yen</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Long</strong> Nikkei {% gl "put option" %}put options{% endgl %} (a hedge)</td><td>Higher rates might knock Japanese shares; the puts pay out if they fall a lot</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The whole trade is sized to risk about \(2\%\) of the fund. Here's how it would have gone if a fund had put it on the day before the BoJ's first surprise.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>The Japan trade, step by step</h3><p>Entry on 19 December 2022. Pick a date to see each leg's profit or loss as a share of the fund, and switch legs on and off. The sizes are illustrative, the prices are rounded, and the puts (bought each December) are counted at what they'd pay out at expiry; the market moves are what really happened.</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="gm-group"><span>Legs in the trade</span><div class="gm-legs" id="jp-legs" aria-label="Legs of the trade"></div></div>
      <div class="gm-group"><span>Jump to</span><div class="presets gm-pick" id="jp-date" aria-label="Date"></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="readout" id="jp-read"></div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-jp" viewBox="0 0 640 200" role="img" aria-label="Profit or loss from each leg of the Japan trade, as a share of the fund, at the chosen date"></svg>
    <div class="legend"><span><i class="sw tone"></i>Made money</span><span><i class="sw grey"></i>Lost money</span></div>
    <p class="look" id="jp-story"></p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>The fund was right about the Bank of Japan, and still lost money for over a year. A currency is a pair: the yen is priced against the dollar, and US interest rates mattered as much as Japan's. A macro view has to be right about the other side of every trade too, and right <em>soon enough</em>.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 5, "Famous Trades and What They Teach", "What did the legendary macro trades have in common?", "Famous trades" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>From Jones to \$4.5 Trillion and Crashes That Changed the Rules tell these stories. Here they're laid side by side as trades, so the common pattern shows.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Trade</th><th>The view</th><th>How it was expressed</th><th>The lesson</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Soros, Black Wednesday (1992)</td><td>The UK couldn't keep the pound inside Europe's exchange rate system during a recession</td><td>A huge short on the pound; about \(\$1\) billion profit</td><td>A {% gl "currency peg" %}currency peg{% endgl %} the economy can't afford tends to break suddenly, and in one direction</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tudor Jones, Black Monday (1987)</td><td>The market looked like 1929, with heavy borrowing and "portfolio insurance" set to sell into falls</td><td>A big short; the fund made about \(126\%\) in October 1987 alone</td><td>Study how the market is built and who will be forced to sell</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paulson, subprime (2007–08)</td><td>House prices were propped up by loans that couldn't be repaid</td><td>CDS on subprime mortgage bonds; about \(\$15\) billion for his firm</td><td>Find bets where the loss is small and fixed and the gain is huge</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Japan (2022–24)</td><td>The BoJ would have to give up yield curve control</td><td>Short JGBs, long yen, Nikkei puts</td><td>Right on policy isn't enough: timing and the other side of the pair decide the result</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The best trades share a shape: <strong>limited downside, large upside</strong>. Shorting a pegged currency risks little while the peg holds and pays a lot if it breaks. A CDS costs a small yearly premium and pays the whole bond if it defaults. Macro managers hunt for that lopsided payoff, because most of their ideas won't work.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 6, "What Goes Wrong", "What are the big risks in macro?", "What goes wrong" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>1. Timing</h3>
    <p>Macro views can take quarters or years to play out. A manager can be right about where a currency or a bond yield will end up, and lose a fortune on the way there. "The market can stay irrational longer than you can stay solvent," as the saying goes. Shorting Japanese bonds was nicknamed the {% gl "widowmaker" %}widowmaker{% endgl %} because so many funds tried it, years too early, from the late 1990s on.</p>
    <h3>2. Leverage and stop-losses</h3>
    <p>Currencies and government bonds usually move a few percent a year, so macro funds borrow to make those moves matter. With leverage, a move the wrong way can hit a fund's {% gl "stop-loss" %}stop-loss{% endgl %} before the view has had time to work. This is why many managers use options: the most an option can lose is what it cost.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Right, but too early</h3><p>Your view is right on average: the price should end the year \(10\%\) higher. But it wobbles on the way. Compare a futures position with a stop-loss against a {% gl "call option" %}call option{% endgl %} that costs a premium up front. Each line is one possible year; the chart shows \(40\) of them, and the numbers come from \(1{,}000\).</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="te-vol">How bumpy</label><input type="range" id="te-vol" min="5" max="30" step="1" value="15"><output id="te-vol-out" for="te-vol">15%/yr</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="te-stop">Stop-loss at</label><input type="range" id="te-stop" min="2" max="20" step="1" value="5"><output id="te-stop-out" for="te-stop">−5%</output></div>
      <div class="presets"><button type="button" id="te-again">Run again</button></div>
    </div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-te" viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-label="Forty possible price paths over a year, with the stop-loss level marked and the paths that hit it shown in grey"></svg>
    <div class="legend"><span><i class="sw tone"></i>Never hit the stop</span><span><i class="sw grey"></i>Stopped out</span></div>
    <div class="readout" id="te-read"></div>
    <p class="look" id="te-story"></p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>3. Policy surprises</h3>
    <p>Central banks and governments can change the rules overnight: a surprise rate move, intervention in the currency, capital controls. On 20 December 2022 the BoJ unexpectedly widened its yield band and the yen jumped about \(3.5\%\) against the dollar in minutes. In 2015 the Swiss National Bank dropped its cap on the franc and the currency jumped about \(30\%\). A carefully built position can be wrecked between one screen refresh and the next.</p>
    <h3>4. Correlations jump in a crisis</h3>
    <p>A macro book might hold FX, bonds, commodities and stock indices that normally move independently. In a panic they can all start moving together as everyone sells risky assets at once, and the "diversified" book behaves like one big bet on whether investors are calm or scared.</p>
    <h3>5. Crowding</h3>
    <p>When most macro funds hold the same view ("long the dollar", "short the yen"), the exit is narrow. For years investors borrowed cheap yen to buy higher-yielding assets, the {% gl "carry trade" %}carry trade{% endgl %}. When the BoJ raised rates on 31 July 2024 and weak US jobs data followed, everyone rushed out together: the dollar fell from about \(155\) to about \(143\) yen in days, and on 5 August the Nikkei dropped \(12.4\%\), its worst day since 1987.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

