  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 8, "Your Three Markets, Macro-Style", "How does a macro fund think about FX, gold and bonds?", "FX, gold and bonds" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>For most strategies this section is a side note. For macro, FX, gold and bonds are the main event.</p>
    <h3>FX: every trade is two countries</h3>
    <p>A macro FX view is always a view on a gap: between two central banks, two growth rates or two trade balances. The Japan trade shows why. Macro funds also run the carry trade deliberately (borrow in a low-rate currency, hold a high-rate one), knowing it earns steadily and then loses fast when it unwinds.</p>
    <h3>Gold: the anti-currency</h3>
    <p>Macro funds treat gold as money that no central bank can print. It tends to do well when real interest rates (after inflation) fall, when the dollar weakens, and when trust in governments wobbles. Central banks themselves have been buying gold heavily since 2022, after Russia's reserves held abroad were frozen, a demand that macro funds now watch closely.</p>
    <h3>Bonds: betting on central banks</h3>
    <p>Most rates trades are bets on what a central bank will do compared with what the market expects. A fund that thinks the Bank of England will cut faster than priced buys short-dated gilts or receives fixed on a swap. Macro funds also trade the shape of the yield curve: betting that long and short yields move closer together or further apart.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 9, "Key Words", "What does the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>The view</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The macro opinion behind a trade.</p><p class="eg">"What's your view on the BoJ?"</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Expression</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The instrument chosen to bet on a view.</p><p class="eg">"Good view, bad expression: the yen leg cost you."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Priced in</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Already reflected in market prices.</p><p class="eg">"Two cuts are priced in; you need three to make money."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Positioning</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>How the market is already betting.</p><p class="eg">"Positioning's stretched; everyone's short yen."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Carry</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>What a position earns just by being held.</p><p class="eg">"The carry pays you to wait."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Risk-off</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Investors selling risky assets and running to safe ones.</p><p class="eg">"Full risk-off: yen, Treasuries and gold all bid."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Asymmetric</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Small possible loss, big possible gain.</p><p class="eg">"Puts on the peg: nicely asymmetric."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Widowmaker</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A famous trade that has ruined many who tried it.</p><p class="eg">"Shorting JGBs was the widowmaker for 20 years."</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 10, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where macro thinking turns up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: macro is your home strategy</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>FX, gold and bonds are macro markets, so this is the hedge fund strategy closest to your own trading. Borrow the framework: write the view, check whether it's priced in (central bank pricing, futures positioning in the CFTC's weekly report), then size by how much of your account you're willing to lose, not by how sure you feel. And keep a journal of being right too early; it happens more than being wrong.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: a macro bet with a building attached</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Buying a holiday villa in Europe with pounds is three bets at once: on local property prices, on interest rates (your mortgage and the buyers' mortgages) and on the euro against the pound. A macro investor would ask which of those she actually has a view on, and hedge or avoid the others, for example by borrowing in euros so the debt and the house share a currency.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: a peg breaking up close</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Nigeria lived through a textbook macro event: in June 2023 the central bank stopped holding the naira at an official rate far stronger than the street price, and the naira lost well over half its value against the dollar over the following months. That's the Black Wednesday pattern: a held price the economy couldn't support, then a sudden one-way move. Tools that help Nigerian businesses and savers see and hedge their currency exposure address exactly the risk macro funds trade.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 11, "The Maths Underneath", "How do you size a macro trade and work out its profit?" %}
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Sizing from a risk budget</span>\[ \text{Notional} = \frac{\text{Risk budget} \times \text{NAV}}{\text{Loss at your stop (as a fraction of notional)}} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">A bond position's profit (short bonds gain when yields rise)</span>\[ \text{P\&L}_{\text{short bond}} \approx D \times \Delta y \times \text{Notional} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">A currency position's profit (long yen against the dollar)</span>\[ \text{P\&L}_{\text{long JPY}} = \left(\frac{S_0}{S_1} - 1\right) \times \text{Notional} \quad (S = \text{yen per dollar}) \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">An option's payoff</span>\[ \text{Call: } \max(S_T - K,\,0) - \text{premium} \qquad \text{Put: } \max(K - S_T,\,0) - \text{premium} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>\(D\) is the bond's {% gl "duration" %}duration{% endgl %}; \(\Delta y\) is the change in yield as a decimal. An at-the-money option on something with yearly volatility \(\sigma\) costs roughly \(0.4\,\sigma\sqrt{T}\) of the notional, a handy rule of thumb.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example</span>
    <h3>A \(\$1\) billion macro fund wants to risk \(2\%\) on shorting 10-year JGBs (duration \(8.5\)). It will close the trade if yields fall \(0.20\) percentage points.</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Risk budget: \(2\% \times \$1\text{bn} = \$20\) million.</li>
      <li>Loss at the stop: \(8.5 \times 0.002 = 1.7\%\) of notional.</li>
      <li>Notional: \(\$20\text{m} \div 0.017 \approx \$1.18\) billion of bonds, more than the whole fund, which is why macro uses leverage.</li>
      <li>If yields instead rise \(0.36\) points (roughly what happened in 2023): \(8.5 \times 0.0036 \times \$1.18\text{bn} \approx \$36\) million, or \(3.6\%\) of the fund.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Sizing comes from the loss you'll accept at the stop, not from how big you'd like the win to be.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 12, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>Explain the difference between discretionary and systematic macro, with an example firm for each.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Discretionary macro relies on a manager's judgement about the economy, with a few concentrated, high-conviction positions (Soros's Quantum Fund, Druckenmiller's Duquesne). Systematic macro and CTAs use models, mostly trend-following, to trade many markets without human calls (Man AHL, Winton, AQR).</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>Name the three steps of the macro framework, and say which one beginners most often skip.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Macro analysis (what will happen), market analysis (is it priced in: positioning, flows, sentiment) and risk management (sizing, correlations, scenarios). Beginners skip market analysis: being right only pays if the market hasn't already priced the view.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>You're long the yen at \(150\) per dollar with \(\$50\) million notional. It moves to \(140\). What's your profit?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\((150 \div 140 - 1) \times \$50\text{m} \approx 7.1\% \times \$50\text{m} \approx \$3.6\) million.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>A \(\$500\) million fund risks \(1.5\%\) on a trade with a stop \(4\%\) away. How big is the position?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Risk budget \(= 1.5\% \times 500 = \$7.5\) million. Notional \(= 7.5 \div 0.04 = \$187.5\) million.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>Why did shorting Japanese bonds earn the nickname "the widowmaker"?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>For decades, funds bet that Japan's huge debts would push its bond yields up, and for decades the BoJ and deflation kept yields near zero. Funds that were right in principle lost money year after year: a lesson in timing risk.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">6</span><span>What happened on 5 August 2024, and what does it teach about crowding?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>After the BoJ raised rates on 31 July and weak US jobs data, investors who had borrowed yen to buy other assets rushed to close the carry trade together. The yen jumped and the Nikkei fell \(12.4\%\) in a day. When everyone holds the same position, the exit is crowded, and the reversal is far faster than the build-up.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">7</span><span>Interview question: give me a macro trade idea.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Structure it in four parts: the view (what will happen and why), why it isn't priced in (what the market expects instead), the expression (which instrument, and why that one rather than others) and the risk (how much you'd lose, where you'd be wrong, and what would make you exit). Pick a market you follow, such as gold or a currency pair, and be ready to defend each part.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}
